Fix the regime where the output length equals the input length $n$, the error is $\varepsilon = 2^{-n}$, and the independence parameter is $k = \mathrm{poly}(n)$ (large enough that Theorem~5.1's standing hypothesis $k > tw/n+1$ holds for the localities $t$ in question). For a word size $w\in\{1\}\cup[\Omega(\log^2 n), n^2]$ -- the range where the paper gives both a lower bound and a matching explicit construction -- the best locality lower bound and the best locality achieved by an explicit construction currently known for an $\varepsilon$-almost $k$-wise independent $t$-word-local family $\mathcal{F} = \{f_s : \{0,1\}^n \to \{0,1\}^n\}_{s \in \Sigma^\ell}$ (Definitions~\ref{def:almost-kwise} and~\ref{def:tlocal}) are
\[ t_{\mathrm{lb}}(n,w) = \Omega\!\left(\frac{n}{\sqrt{w}\,\log n}\right), \qquad t_{\mathrm{ub}}(n,w) = O\!\left(\frac{n}{\sqrt{w}}\right), \]
which differ by a multiplicative factor of $\Theta(\log n)$.

Determine the true order of the minimal locality $t^*(n,w)$ achievable by an explicit $\varepsilon$-almost $k$-wise independent $t$-word-local family in this regime, by resolving one of the following for some $w\in\{1\}\cup[\Omega(\log^2 n), n^2]$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] exhibit an explicit $\varepsilon$-almost $k$-wise independent $t$-word-local family with $t = o(n/\sqrt{w})$, improving the construction; or
\item[(b)] prove that every $\varepsilon$-almost $k$-wise independent $t$-word-local family, explicit or not, must have $t = \Omega(n/\sqrt{w})$, improving the lower bound;
\end{itemize}
thereby closing the $\Theta(\log n)$ gap between $t_{\mathrm{lb}}$ and $t_{\mathrm{ub}}$ for at least one such $w$. (This statement does not cover the intermediate range $2\le w=o(\log^2 n)$, where the paper gives no matching construction to compare the lower bound against in the first place.)
